      "sha256": "6f05e38e3f17fde530f4d881e6de4eb21147f1966b1dfbb06ef07dbf8b95deb9",
      "kind": "figure",
      "alt": "Figure 1. Index of methods used in participant code.1: create a new dictionary or array of letters/numbers; example initializes an empty list, loops over data, and appends even values.2: loop to find the maximum length using an accumulator.3: use min or max to find the word or number.4: sort, with a lambda function.5: loop with an accumulator and an if condition directly solving the question; example updates the smallest even value.6: use a filter function. These indices identify methods in Figure 2."
    },
    {
      "path": "figures/xu-how-pairing-code-chilbw2023/figure-003-p004.png",
      "node": "399:0",
      "page": 4,
      "bbox": [
        51.79800033569336,
        81.68621063232422,
        296.0444641113281,
        203.98300170898438
      ],
      "sha256": "79a5e0e2cb0d7c01b713d1302c9c9d9fe0e95b75784d6a209681c4b73267bc95",
      "kind": "figure",
      "alt": "Figure 2. Methods used for questions 1 and 2, using indices defined in Figure 1. D denotes different-solution groups, shaded gray; S denotes similar-solution groups, white. Each row below gives question 1, then question 2. D1a: 2, partially solved; 1, 2, 4, 5. D1b: 1, 2; data lost. D2a: 1, 3; 1, 2, 4, 5. D2b: 1, 2; 1, 2. D3a: 3, partially solved; 1, 3. D3b: 1, 4; 1, 4, 6. D4a and D4b: failed to solve both questions. S1a: 5; 2, 4, 5. S1b: 5; 5. S2a: 5; 1, 4. S2b: 3, partially solved; 3, 6. S3a and S3b: 5; 1, 4, 5. S3c: 5; 3, 4, 5."
    },
    {
      "path": "figures/xu-how-pairing-code-chilbw2023/figure-004-p004.png",
      "node": "404:0",
      "page": 4,
      "bbox": [
        339.97900390625,
        81.68405151367188,
        536.1660766601562,
        469.1180114746094
      ],
      "sha256": "1d6686831d6b0ff29e8486a345460b663dfbed7330777bcea9e7fb8e6469ce21",
      "kind": "figure",
      "alt": "Figure 3. Three examples show reasoning expressed in discussion but missing from submitted code; red text marks those additional thoughts. Example 1: a participant considered sorting, rejected alphabetical sorting, and used a loop that compares string lengths to find the longest word. Example 2: a participant wanted to collect strings ending in e before taking a maximum but could not implement the filtering; the submitted code only returns max(data). Example 3: a participant intended to use endswith and retrieve the corresponding value but ran out of time; the submitted code returns data[3]."
    }
